# Line endings
# ============
#
# Everything in this repository is LF, in the repository and in the working
# copy, on Windows as much as anywhere else. Without this file Git on Windows
# follows core.autocrlf, which defaults to true there, and converts every text
# file to CRLF on checkout - which is what makes GitHub Desktop warn that a file
# "uses LF but Git is configured to convert them to CRLF".
#
# This is not only cosmetic. Two of the files here are read by programs that
# care:
#
#   - the Makefile. GNU make under MSYS2 keeps the carriage return at the end of
#     a recipe line and hands it to the shell, so a converted Makefile fails
#     with errors that name a command nobody wrote;
#   - .github/workflows/build.yml, whose `run:` blocks are shell scripts and
#     have the same problem inside the msys2 shell.
#
# Settling it here rather than in each machine's git config is also what stops
# the answer from depending on who cloned the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Bytes, not text: Git must not rewrite anything inside these.
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.bmp binary
*.ttf binary
